<Base
  title="Roadmap — Tuile"
  description="Tuile's milestones, what each one delivered as of v0.14.0, what was built outside them, and what is in review or planned."
  kicker="Roadmap"
  heading="Milestones, and where each one actually stands."
  lede="A milestone is finished when every one of its criteria passes — not when the code exists. This page says what was delivered as of v0.14.0, what changed course, and what has not started; the per-criterion state is in the repository's docs/03-roadmap.md."
>
  <p class="note">
    <strong>The north star</strong> is one foundation that serves every mode — interactive
    on desktop, phone, headset and browser, films, offline renderers. The structuring target
    so far has been a rendered globe: streamed terrain draped with raster imagery, end to end
    in Rust, on top of the 3D Tiles engine.
  </p>

  <h2>Where things stand</h2>

  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Milestone</th><th>Status</th><th>In short</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {status.map(([m, kind, label, what]) => (
        <tr><td>{m}</td><td><span class={`tag ${kind}`}>{label}</span></td><td>{what}</td></tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>MG — the globe</h2>

  <div class="milestone">
    <h3>Quantized-mesh terrain and draped imagery <span class="tag shipping">delivered</span></h3>
    <p>
      Terrain decoding and encoding — header, delta-encoded vertices, high-water-mark
      indices, skirts, octahedral normals — producing the same decoded content type a glTF
      tile produces. A geographic quadtree with per-level availability. A source abstraction
      that makes the traversal, the budget and the geometry server generic over where tiles
      come from. Imagery providers, projections, drapes as resources of their own, several
      layers each falling back on its own. Sources from connectors, from any terrain
      <code>layer.json</code> and imagery template, or from a heightfield served over plain
      HTTP with no token at all.
    </p>
    <p class="crit">
      <strong>Done means:</strong> the traversal refines terrain by screen-space error with no
      terrain-specific selection code, and a build with no provider credentials works
      against a free source — both checked. Decoding a real terrain tile, a jitter-free globe
      at every scale and attributions on screen are met in the viewers, and checked by eye
      rather than by a test.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>M1 — core and reference renderer</h2>

  <div class="milestone">
    <h3>The MVP that proves everything else is possible <span class="tag shipping">core delivered</span></h3>
    <p>
      Full parsing of the tile tree in both versions of the standard, external tilesets
      included. Content decoding for both container generations. A pure, tested traversal:
      frustum culling, replace-with-hold-until-ready, additive refinement, request
      prioritisation. The protocol module — the message types, the two content forms, the
      <code>GeometryStream</code> trait and its in-process binding. Fetchers for the
      filesystem and for HTTP. The wgpu backend with relative-to-centre rendering.
    </p>
    <p class="crit">
      <strong>Met:</strong> a green test run on Linux and macOS with traversal coverage on a
      hand-written mini-tileset; a green WebAssembly check; no <code>unwrap</code> or
      <code>panic</code> on the core's production paths, denied by lint in CI.
      <strong>Not met:</strong> an interactive viewer that opens a tileset — tilesets are
      drawn by an offscreen snapshot — and the committed sample tilesets it would be checked
      against.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>M2 — network middleware</h2>

  <div class="milestone">
    <h3>Streaming over a network, static mode, a removable cache <span class="tag planned">not started</span></h3>
    <p>
      The network binding of the streaming protocol: one session per consumer that declares
      its memory, one geometry server and budget per session, a message envelope versioned
      from its first byte, geometry serialised as glb; its transport is still an open
      decision. A native serving binary pointed at a directory or at object storage. A cache
      that can be switched off entirely when something upstream already caches. Correct HTTP
      semantics for the static mode: entity tags, immutable cache control on content, range
      requests, configurable cross-origin policy. An edge build of the same service, sharing
      the routes rather than forking them.
    </p>
    <p class="crit">
      <strong>Done will mean:</strong> a remote viewer that matches the local one in its
      <em>selection sequence</em> for a replayed camera path — a programmatic test, not an
      eyeball; independent open-source 3D Tiles web viewers consuming a served tileset
      without error; a session's budget held within the memory its consumer declared.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>M2.5 — raster imagery in the core</h2>

  <div class="milestone">
    <h3>Because geometry needs something draped over it <span class="tag shipping">delivered for terrain</span></h3>
    <p>
      An imagery-provider trait and its implementations, web-mercator and geographic grids,
      imagery tiles picked at a density matching the geometry's screen-space error and
      reprojected once, drapes delivered and budgeted on their own. Since v0.13, imagery can
      also be stored as KTX2 and held block-compressed on the GPU — the client's choice, off
      by default.
    </p>
    <p class="crit">
      <strong>Met</strong> for terrain: draped, attributed, its memory budgeted.
      <strong>Not done:</strong> draping an untextured 3D Tiles tileset.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>M3 — USD</h2>

  <div class="milestone">
    <h3>From exporting geometry to rendering inside OpenUSD <span class="tag shipping">redirected</span></h3>
    <p>
      The plan was a packaged-archive exporter: a bounding box and a target error in, a
      portable scene out. What shipped follows where the need went — rendering a recorded
      flight inside an offline renderer. A USD stage of a few kilobytes, written as text in
      pure Rust, carries the camera path and one globe prim; a Hydra plugin over a C ABI
      resolves the stage's camera and streams terrain and imagery into whichever Hydra
      renderer draws. The exporter is not started; its bar — opening cleanly in standard USD
      and mobile viewers, deterministic, bounded — stands if it is picked up. The server
      never knows USD.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>M4 and beyond</h2>

  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>The scene-index integration</strong> <span class="tag shipping">shipped</span>
      — linked in-process through a C ABI, one frame at a time, rather than over a socket.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Game engines</strong> — <code>tuile-bevy</code>
      <span class="tag review">in review</span>, the globe inside a Rust game engine on
      <code>tuile-wgpu</code>'s host pass; further increments planned.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A platform-native façade for phones, tablets and headsets</strong>
      <span class="tag planned">not started</span> — phones and tablets run the wgpu viewer
      today; a headset is what would force it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>JavaScript bindings of the core</strong>
      <span class="tag planned">not started</span> — the browser runs the whole viewer as
      an ES module today.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A control plane</strong> for authentication, indexing and streamed
      invalidations <span class="tag planned">not started</span> — never a replacement for
      the HTTP data plane.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Tile generation on the fly</strong> — begun for terrain (any heightfield
      served as quantized-mesh tiles); columnar geospatial sources not started.
    </li>
    <li><strong>Point clouds and gaussian splats</strong> <span class="tag planned">not started</span>.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    There is no generic foreign-function facade and there will not be one. Each host gets a
    facade shaped like that host's own idioms, because a lowest-common-denominator C API
    would be worse for every one of them.
  </p>

  <h2>Built outside the milestones</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>The graphics budget</strong> — one pure, deterministic decision per step for viewers and films alike, simulated in CI.</li>
    <li><strong>Four viewer shells of one application</strong> — desktop, iOS, Android and the web.</li>
    <li><strong>Embedding</strong> — a viewer generic over its sources, a host that places the camera, a renderer that draws into a frame it does not own.</li>
    <li><strong>Films</strong> — record, bake once, render often, on a farm; imagery harmonised to one light; a physical sky with sun and moon.</li>
    <li><strong>A source-tile store</strong> and its read API.</li>
    <li><strong>GPU-compressed imagery</strong> — the client's choice, off by default.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Next</h2>

  <p>None of this is on <code>main</code>; it is listed so the direction is visible, not promised.</p>

  <ul>
    <li><span class="tag review">in review</span> <code>tuile-bevy</code>, first increment.</li>
    <li><span class="tag planned">draft</span> The viewer's physical atmosphere of the current time, shared with the film.</li>
    <li><span class="tag planned">planned</span> The compression budget: the GPU time and transient memory of encoding accounted inside the core budget.</li>
    <li><span class="tag planned">planned</span> Compact vertex encoding (meshopt) read by the core's glTF decoder, always on, in pure Rust.</li>
    <li><span class="tag planned">exploring</span> Procedural terrain with unbounded detail.</li>
  </ul>

